#: src/idiomatic/leveraging-the-type-system/typestate-pattern/typestate-advanced.md:82
msgid ""
"Building on our previous serializer, we now want to support **nested "
"structures** and **lists**."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/leveraging-the-type-system/typestate-pattern/typestate-advanced.md:85
msgid ""
"However, this introduces both **duplication** and **structural complexity**."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/leveraging-the-type-system/typestate-pattern/typestate-advanced.md:87
msgid ""
"Even more critically, we now hit a **type system limitation**: we cannot "
"cleanly express what `finish()` should return without duplicating variants "
"for every nesting context (e.g. root, struct, list)."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/leveraging-the-type-system/typestate-pattern/typestate-advanced.md:91
msgid "From the diagram of valid transitions, we can observe:"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/leveraging-the-type-system/typestate-pattern/typestate-advanced.md:92
msgid "The transitions are recursive"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/leveraging-the-type-system/typestate-pattern/typestate-advanced.md:93
msgid "The return types depend on _where_ a substructure or list appears"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/leveraging-the-type-system/typestate-pattern/typestate-advanced.md:94
msgid "Each context requires a return path to its parent"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/leveraging-the-type-system/typestate-pattern/typestate-advanced.md:96
msgid ""
"With only concrete types, this becomes unmanageable. Our current approach "
"leads to an explosion of types and manual wiring."
msgstr ""

